  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nOn Monday, a cold front will move south along the coast of NC \nduring peak heating. West to northwest low-level flow should \ntend to limit the strength of convergence along the front. \nHowever, as the front encounters the developing seabreeze, there\nshould be a local enhancement to low-level forcing, especially \nalong and south of HWY 70. This is the area where the best \nchance for sustained, deep convection exists. The risk is \nconditional on sufficient low-level forcing, but should this \noccur, a short-lived risk of strong to marginally severe \nthunderstorms may develop thanks to elevated deep layer shear \n(~30kt) and moderate instability (MLCAPE up to 1500j/kg). \nMachine learning guidance isn't all that excited about this \npotential, and this likely speaks to the conditionality of the \nthreat. This risk will quickly end from north to south as the \nfront shifts off the Crystal Coast by sunset.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Guidance continue to trend stronger with an \nupper level ridge that will be expanding across the Southern \nU.S. this week. In fact, ensemble guidance is now forecasting \n500mb heights around 600dm with this ridge, the strength of \nwhich is very unusual for this time of year. Normally, this \nwould spell anomalous heat for ENC. However, a pocket of \n\"cooler\" thicknesses is forecast to be over the Carolinas \nassociated with the post-frontal northeasterly flow regime, \nespecially Tuesday and Wednesday. While it will be dry, those \ncooler thicknesses may shield ENC from the anomalous heat that \nother parts of the South will experience. That pocket of lower \nthicknesses may begin to erode by the end of the week, with \ntemperatures warming by then. However, guidance differ on \nwhen/if this will occur.",
